{"id":229,"date":"2017-07-11T22:13:48","date_gmt":"2017-07-11T22:13:48","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/?page_id=229"},"modified":"2019-03-25T11:14:48","modified_gmt":"2019-03-25T11:14:48","slug":"code","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/code\/","title":{"rendered":"Code &#038; Examples"},"content":{"rendered":"<h2>Example Service<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/exampleservice.domainconnect.org\">https:\/\/exampleservice.domainconnect.org<\/a><\/p>\n<p>To aid developers in seeing how to use Domain Connect, we&#8217;ve built a simple example hosting service. This can be used by DNS Providers to test their implementation of Domain Connect, or by Service Providers to see how to implement Domain Connect.<\/p>\n<p>The source code for this application can be found at <a href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/python-dc-statelesshosting\">https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/python-dc-statelesshosting<\/a><\/p>\n<p>This application is a simple Python Bottle application. It implements both the synchronous (with and without a signature) and asynchronous protocols using OAuth.<\/p>\n<p>The application implements a fictitious hosting company called &#8220;Stateless Hosting&#8221;. \u00a0The &#8220;consumer&#8221; enters their domain name and the text they want displayed on their website. Using Domain Connect, DNS is configured with the proper IP address for the apex A record. The message is also cleverly stored in DNS in a TXT record. Yes, we are using DNS as a distributed database and we don&#8217;t necessarily advocate this in real practice.<\/p>\n<h2>Signature Tools<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/exampleservice.domainconnect.org\/sig\">https:\/\/exampleservice.domainconnect.org\/sig<\/a><\/p>\n<p>One of the trickier things to get right as a Service Provider is when signatures are used to verify synchronous calls. Testing that the signature is generated properly often involves guess work and back and forth with the DNS Providers.<\/p>\n<p>We&#8217;ve built a simple testing tool as part of the example service.<\/p>\n<p>We also built a tool that can generate the necessary DNS TXT record values for publishing the values in DNS.<\/p>\n<h2>DNS Providers<\/h2>\n<p>We have some code that should help the DNS Provider get going. The github project <a href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/DomainConnectApplyZone\">DomainConnectApplyZone<\/a> has code that takes a zone file and can apply a template. It handles conflict detection and signature checks, returning a new version of the zone file.<\/p>\n<p>It doesn&#8217;t implement any authentication or UX, but does implement the core of applying a template.<\/p>\n<h2>Dynamic DNS<\/h2>\n<p>As another example project we&#8217;ve built a Dynamic DNS Windows Service. This service uses the asynchronous OAuth protocol, and will keep a DNS A record current with a dynamic IP address assigned to a home network.<\/p>\n<p>While similar protocols are built into some routers, these are proprietary. This application will work with any DNS Provider that supports the OAuth protocol and the service template.<\/p>\n<p>The source code for this project can be found at:<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/DomainConnectDDNS\">https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/DomainConnectDDNS<\/a><\/p>\n<p>The compiled version can be found at:<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/DomainConnectDDNS\/blob\/master\/DomainConnectDDNS.zip\">https:\/\/github.com\/Domain-Connect\/DomainConnectDDNS\/blob\/master\/DomainConnectDDNS.zip<\/a><\/p>\n<h2>Plesk Extension<\/h2>\n<p>Plesk has built an extension that allows Plesk to operate as a DNS Provider (when Plesk is running DNS for the domain).<\/p>\n<p>The code can be found at:<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/plesk\/ext-domain-connect\">https:\/\/github.com\/plesk\/ext-domain-connect<\/a><\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Example Service https:\/\/exampleservice.domainconnect.org To aid developers in seeing how to use Domain Connect, we&#8217;ve built a simple example hosting service.&hellip;<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":3,"featured_media":0,"parent":0,"menu_order":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","template":"","meta":{"footnotes":""},"class_list":["post-229","page","type-page","status-publish","hentry"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/229","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/page"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/3"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=229"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/229\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":454,"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/229\/revisions\/454"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/www.domainconnect.org\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=229"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}